      imageAlt: "",
      text: `This original dramatic or longer-fiction excerpt uses dialogue, pacing, and symbolic objects to reveal conflict.\n\n[A narrow parlor. Evening. A sealed envelope lies on the mantel. Beatrice stands beside the window while Esther remains near the door.]\n\nBeatrice: You came early.\nEsther: I came before the house could fill with advice.\nBeatrice: Advice is only a command that has learned manners.\nEsther: Then you have been commanded kindly all your life.\n\n[Beatrice turns toward the mantel but does not touch the envelope.]\n\nBeatrice: My father believed a closed letter was proof of patience.\nEsther: Your father believed many things that required other people to be silent.\nBeatrice: And yet here you are, asking me to speak as if speech were not also a kind of inheritance.\n\nThe clock strikes once. Neither character moves. The envelope remains where it is, brighter than the lamp beside it.`,
